          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p15\",\"quote\":\"Expeditions run up to six months, from launch until undocking\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p15\",\"quote\":\"With the arrival of crew on larger US commercial spacecraft beginning in 2020, crew size has been increased to seven, the number for which ISS was originally designed.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p19\",\"quote\":\"Flights to the ISS have included 95 Progress, 73 Soyuz, 52 SpaceX Dragon , 37 Space Shuttle, 22 Cygnus , 10 HTV / HTV-X , 5 ATV , and 2 Boeing Starliner missions.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p36\",\"quote\":\"Resupply missions typically use the Russian Progress spacecraft, i.e., Progress-M (Standard and Modified), Progress-M1 and Progress MS series vehicles, European Automated Transfer Vehicles , Japanese Kounotori vehicles, and the American Dragon 1 and 2 vehicles and Cygnus (Standard, Enhanced and XL series) spacecraft.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p37\",\"quote\":\"Since construction started, the International Space Station program has had to deal with several maintenance issues, unexpected problems and failures.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p38\",\"quote\":\"The components of the ISS are operated and monitored by their respective space agencies at mission control centers across the globe.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p47\",\"quote\":\"Crews are launched to the station via Russian Soyuz missions and US launch vehicles, although the US operated none between the 2011 Space Shuttle retirement and 2018 first crewed launch of SpaceX Dragon 2 .\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p47\",\"quote\":\"The station has been resupplied by cargo spacecraft operated by the US, Russia, European Space Agency , and Japan.\"}]}"
